  "body": {
    "id": "prop:endpoint-degeneracy",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every \\(\\vartheta\\in\\Theta\\) and \\(S\\in\\mathcal A\\), \\(\\nu_S^2(\\vartheta)=0\\) if and only if \\(b_S(\\vartheta)=0\\), equivalently \\(\\mu_0=\\mu_1\\), \\(\\Sigma_{SS}^{-1}c_{0,S}=\\Sigma_{SS}^{-1}c_{1,S}\\), and \\(r_0(S)=r_1(S)\\). Hence every equality parameter is excluded from \\(\\Theta_+(\\nu_*)\\) for every fixed \\(\\nu_*>0\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "vartheta",
      "Theta",
      "S",
      "Acal",
      "nuS",
      "btheta",
      "mu0",
      "mu1",
      "Sigma",
      "c0",
      "c1",
      "r0",
      "r1",
      "ThetaPlus",
      "nuStar"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:moment-parameterization",
      "def:endpoint-influence",
      "def:wald-separated-image",
      "prop:gaussian-endpoint",
      "prop:parameter-image-regularity"
    ],
    "justification": "The equivalence isolates the quadratic equality boundary and makes the domains of ordinary Wald and projection inference disjoint where first-order studentization fails.",
    "gap": "Lin et al. identify equality as a degeneracy point for a fixed quadratic transport target; this proposition records the exact Gaussian selected-endpoint criterion needed to route inference, without claiming a new generic selection principle.",
    "consumer": "Analysts can determine from the moment parameter whether to use separated Wald inference or the whole-image projection interval.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(S\\in\\mathcal A\\) and abbreviate\n\\(\\Delta_\\mu=\\mu_1-\\mu_0\\), \\(\\beta_a=\\Sigma_{SS}^{-1}c_{a,S}\\),\n\\(t=\\sqrt{r_1(S)/r_0(S)}\\), and\n\\(w=\\sqrt t\\,\\beta_0-\\beta_1/\\sqrt t\\).  The endpoint formula in\n\\(\\texttt{prop:gaussian-endpoint}\\) is a sum of three nonnegative quantities, and \\(\\Sigma_{SS}\\succ0\\).  Consequently\n\\[\n b_S(\\vartheta)=0\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\Delta_\\mu=0,\\quad \\beta_0=\\beta_1,\\quad r_0(S)=r_1(S).\n\\]\nUnder these three equalities, \\(t=1\\) and \\(w=0\\), so every term in the defined influence function vanishes and \\(\\nu_S^2(\\vartheta)=0\\).\n\nConversely, suppose \\(\\nu_S^2(\\vartheta)=0\\).  The influence function is centered, hence it equals zero almost surely.  Conditional on either treatment arm, \\((x_S,e_a)\\) has a strictly positive Gaussian density on \\(\\mathbb R^{|S|+1}\\), because the sparse-spectrum and residual-floor assumptions give \\(\\Sigma_{SS}\\succ0\\) and \\(r_a(S)>0\\).  The arm-specific expression for the influence function is a polynomial of degree at most two in \\((x_S,e_a)\\).  A polynomial vanishing almost surely under a positive density vanishes identically.\n\nIn arm zero, the coefficient of \\(e_0^2\\) is \\((1-t)/(1-\\pi)\\); overlap therefore forces \\(t=1\\).  With \\(t=1\\), the coefficient vector of \\(x_Se_0\\) is \\(2w/(1-\\pi)\\), so \\(w=0\\), and then the coefficient of \\(e_0\\) is \\(-2\\Delta_\\mu/(1-\\pi)\\), so \\(\\Delta_\\mu=0\\).  The relations \\(t=1\\) and \\(w=0\\) are respectively \\(r_0(S)=r_1(S)\\) and \\(\\beta_0=\\beta_1\\).  This proves the equivalence.  By the definition of \\(\\Theta_+(\\nu_*)\\), any parameter with \\(b_S=0\\) for a selectable \\(S\\) has minimum selectable influence standard deviation zero and is excluded for every \\(\\nu_*>0\\).",
